  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Easterly gap winds during the overnights through Sunday\n   morning: choppy lakes and strong canyon winds.\n\n-  Widespread rain and mountain snow above 7,000 feet late\n   Saturday night, through Sunday, into Monday.\n\n-  Drying and warming conditions by mid-week.\n\nIsolated showers will continue over high terrain east of Highway \n93 this afternoon before diminishing. An easterly surface pressure\ngradient will build tonight and persist through Sunday morning, \ngenerating east-to-northeast wind gusts toward 30 mph through \nfavored canyons, on the west slopes of major mountain ranges, and\non regional lakes including Flathead Lake. Saturday through the\nearly evening will remain mostly dry and slightly warmer than\nFriday ahead of the main incoming system.\n\nA closed upper-level low tracking across the region will push \nwidespread showers and embedded thunderstorms from west to east \nstarting after midnight Saturday night and continuing through \nSunday. Recent model guidance has shifted the low track slightly \nfarther north, also slightly increasing precipitation forecasts \nacross north- central Idaho and northwest Montana where 0.50 to \n1.25 inches of rainfall is expected. Southwest Montana will remain\non the drier periphery and within a fetch of elevated west and\nsouthwest winds, again fostering near-critical fire weather\nconditions based solely off of RH and winds. Snow levels drop to \naround 7,000 feet Sunday into Monday morning, creating wet, cold, \nand raw conditions in the backcountry increasing the risk for\nhypothermia to those who are unprepared.\n\nThe low exits east by Tuesday, yielding to a split-flow pattern \naloft by mid-week. Temperatures will rebound toward and above seasonal\naverages alongside a general drying trend, though minor embedded \ndisturbances will maintain low-end shower chances.",
